Hi, 

I'm running into problems trying to install Alpha Core 1.0.  The box is a 
UP2000+ with an Adaptec 39160 controller.  There are (2) drives attached, 
one on each channel.  The dka device is partitioned and formatted for 
Tru64 Unix.  Dkb has (3) other Linux distributions installed and has been 
partitioned with a BSD disklabel scheme.

I'm trying to install onto an unused partition on dkb.  The installer 
trips over dka, telling me that the partition table is unreadable and 
asking if I want to reinitialize it.  I answer "no".  It then tells me 
that dkb is unreadable as well! When I tell it not to initialize that 
disk, it terminates with an error (thinking that no disks are available).

The question is: why can't the installer read the partition information on 
either drive?  Granted, it won't know about AdvFS on dka, but it still 
should have read the disklabel.  And, since the Linux distros on dkb are 
functional there's no reason it should have problems of any sort.

Any advice as to how I proceed with installation and avoid repartitioning 
the second disk?

Steve



From tedgoodridgejr at acm.org  Wed May 11 01:23:27 2005
From: tedgoodridgejr at acm.org (Ted Goodridge, Jr)
Date: Tue, 10 May 2005 20:23:27 -0500
Subject: which way to orient an IDE cable on an LX164
Message-ID: <opsqk7hdpwz218jt@farpoint.ok.cox.net>

I found the IDE connectors, but as there is no notch I was curious to know  
which side the red stripe goes on withi the chip oriented above the IDE  
connectors.  I'm offlist so please CC me directly.

Thanks,
Ted



From ayoung at teleport.com  Wed May 11 02:47:26 2005
From: ayoung at teleport.com (Alan Young)
Date: Tue, 10 May 2005 19:47:26 -0700
Subject: which way to orient an IDE cable on an LX164
In-Reply-To: <opsqk7hdpwz218jt@farpoint.ok.cox.net>
References: <opsqk7hdpwz218jt@farpoint.ok.cox.net>
Message-ID: <20050510194726.73b3c4d2.ayoung@teleport.com>

On the LX, Pin 1 is on the side nearest to the PCI slots.  On the board next to the
connector there's a tiny "1" to indicate where PIN 1 is.  Connect the IDE cable
so that the stripe on the cable is on the same side as the "1".

Alan

P.S. The CMD 646 IDE chip that is on the board is a horribly slow chip.

